<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Operational systems proof</p>
      <h1>Different operational inputs → one manageable workflow</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The public-facing questions can change with the job. The admin layer can still bring those requests together, preserve context and give staff one place to decide what happens next.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="workflow-inputs">
      <% bookingTypes.forEach((type) => { %>
        <article class="workflow-input-card">
          <span class="type-badge"><%= type.shortLabel %></span>
          <h3><%= type.label %></h3>
          <p><%= type.summary %></p>
        </article>
      <% }); %>
    </div>

    <div class="workflow-converge" aria-hidden="true">
      <span>↓</span><strong>Shared operational queue</strong><span>↓</span>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-grid">
      <article class="timeline-card">
        <span class="step-number">01</span>
        <h3>Structured intake</h3>
        <p>The form asks for information appropriate to the request instead of forcing every user through the same fields.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="timeline-card">
        <span class="step-number">02</span>
        <h3>Availability / review logic</h3>
        <p>Resource-led requests can be checked for sample clashes while group requests can stay deliberately review-led.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="timeline-card">
        <span class="step-number">03</span>
        <h3>Shared admin queue</h3>
        <p>All request types appear together, with filters for operational status and intake type.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="timeline-card">
        <span class="step-number">04</span>
        <h3>Status handling</h3>
        <p>Requests move through simple operational states such as New, Availability checked, Confirmed, Follow-up, Completed or Cancelled.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="timeline-card">
        <span class="step-number">05</span>
        <h3>Records & financial context</h3>
        <p>Where appropriate, accepted work can carry forward into operational records and invoice visibility rather than ending at the form submission.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="timeline-card">
        <span class="step-number">06</span>
        <h3>Reporting & export</h3>
        <p>Structured data can be summarised and exported so the organisation keeps control of its information.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="workflow-callout">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Design principle</p>
        <h2>Adapt the intake. Keep the operations coherent.</h2>
        <p>
          This is not presented as an off-the-shelf booking product. It is a reusable proof of how ASR can model different operational processes without overbuilding before the real rules are understood.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/book">Try an intake flow</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/admin">Open shared admin</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
